77,"Pull the new image and recreate the container:"],[1063,"Pulsarr adds your Plex friends as users on its own. These pages set what each user can do and mark their requests in Sonarr, Radarr and Plex."],[71,"Pulsarr API"],[773,"Pulsarr can add exclusions for media that Maintainerr deletes, so stale watchlists don't request it again. It needs Maintainerr 3.23.0 or later, and Maintainerr must be able to reach Pulsarr."],[443,"Pulsarr can only read the watchlist of someone who is a friend of your Plex account. People with access to your server who aren't friends yet show up under **Not yet friends** on the **Plex users** page, where you can send them a friend request."],[1100,"Pulsarr can only read the watchlists of your Plex friends. People with access to your server who aren't friends, and pending friend requests, show under **Not yet friends**."],[914,"Pulsarr can push a notification straight to a user's Plex app on iOS or Android when something on their watchlist becomes available. Tapping it opens the title in Plex. It needs Plex Pass on your server."],[661,"Pulsarr creates the tables, but not the database. Create an empty database and give your user full rights on it first:"],[522,"Pulsarr generates the secrets on first start and stores them, so they stay the same across restarts. Set them in `.env` only if you need fixed values."],[636,"Pulsarr installs to `C:\\Program Files\\Pulsarr`. Where files live:"],[611,"Pulsarr is also in the Unraid Community Applications and TrueNAS community app catalogs. Search for Pulsarr in either one."],[691,"Pulsarr keeps its database and logs in `./data`."],[493,"Pulsarr reads these from `.env`, or from the container's environment in Docker. Most of them are also settings in the app, and you only need `.env` to fix a value before first start or to keep it under version control."],[1053,"Pulsarr reads watchlists through the Plex account that owns your server. To link a different account, select **Relink with Plex** and approve Pulsarr on plex.tv, or **Paste a token** if you already have one."],[686,"Pulsarr reads your own watchlist and the watchlists of your Plex friends. Anyone who isn't a friend of your Plex account is skipped."],[432,"Pulsarr reads your own watchlist and those of your Plex friends. It adds friends as users by itself, the first time it sees them, with your new user defaults. People with access to your server who aren't friends yet show on Plex users, where you can send them a friend request."],[474,"Pulsarr runs as the user and group set by `PUID` and `PGID`, which both default to `1000`. On start it takes ownership of anything in `./data` that has a different owner. Set them to your user on the host, which `id -u` and `id -g` print."],[446,"Pulsarr runs in Docker or as a native install on Linux, macOS and Windows, and stores its data in SQLite or PostgreSQL."],[991,"Pulsarr sends most watchlist items straight to Sonarr or Radarr. An item waits in the approval queue instead when the user requires approval, when it puts them over their quota, or when a Content router route asks for approval."],[421,"Pulsarr sits between Plex watchlists and Sonarr and Radarr. Your users never use Pulsarr directly. They add titles to their watchlist in the Plex app, and Pulsarr decides what to request, where to send it, and who to tell when it's ready."],[903,"Pulsarr tells users when something they asked for is ready, and tells admins about requests, deletions and updates. Pick the channels that suit you."],[438,"Pulsarr turns Plex watchlists into Sonarr and Radarr requests."],[478,"Pulsarr updates its database on start, so there's nothing else to run."],[861,"Pulsarr uses Apprise's stateless notify endpoint, so every URL you enter in Pulsarr is sent along with each notification, and nothing has to be saved in Apprise."],[660,"Pulsarr uses SQLite by default and needs no setup for it. You can use PostgreSQL instead, either from the first start or by moving an existing SQLite install across."